  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Trigohowilol G may interact with Macrophage-expressed gene 1 protein through a series of molecular targets. Specifically, Trigohowilol G is predicted to target DOHH-2, which in turn is a target of Porsapogenin 7 and of (1S,3S)-6,7-dihydroxy-1-methyl-1,2,3,4-tetrahydroisoquinoline-3-carboxylic acid. These compounds are all implicated in the regulation of macrophage function, as they are known to influence cellular processes associated with immune response and inflammation. The indirect pathway through DOHH-2 and Porsapogenin 7 provides a plausible mechanism by which Trigohowilol G could modulate macrophage-expressed gene 1 protein, potentially affecting its role in immune signaling or inflammatory responses. The evidence is consistent with a regulatory or signaling relationship, but the exact nature of the interaction remains uncertain due to the complexity of the molecular pathways involved."
